---
import MainLayout from '../../layouts/MainLayout.astro';

// Held as strings: a bare `{` in .astro markup opens a JS expression, so the JSON
// in these curl examples was compiled as JavaScript and broke the build.
const loginCurl = `curl -X POST http://localhost:5000/api/v1/auth/login \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -c cookies.txt \\
  -d '{
    "email": "user@example.com",
    "password": "YourPassword123"
  }'`;

const sessionCurl = `# Reuse the cookie jar the login wrote
curl -b cookies.txt http://localhost:5000/api/v1/records/contact`;

const apiKeyCurl = `curl -H "X-API-Key: $SALESDAM_API_KEY" \\
  http://localhost:5000/api/v1/records/contact`;
---

<MainLayout title="Authentication | Salesdam Docs" description="Authentication methods and security">
  <div class="content-wrapper">
    <h1>Authentication</h1>

    <p class="intro">Learn about different authentication methods available in Salesdam and how to secure your account.</p>

    <h2>Authentication Methods</h2>

    <h3>Email & Password</h3>
    <p>Standard email and password login.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Email address (username)</li>
      <li>Password (minimum 8 characters)</li>
      <li>Account verification required</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Two-Factor Authentication (2FA)</h3>
    <p>Add an extra layer of security to your account.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Time-based One-Time Password (TOTP)</li>
      <li>SMS verification</li>
      <li>Backup codes for account recovery</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>API Authentication</h3>
    <p>For programmatic access to the API:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>JWT (JSON Web Tokens)</li>
      <li>API Keys</li>
      <li>Token refresh mechanism</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Login Process</h2>

    <ol>
      <li>Navigate to Salesdam login page</li>
      <li>Enter email address</li>
      <li>Enter password</li>
      <li>If 2FA enabled, enter code from authenticator or SMS</li>
      <li>Click "Sign In"</li>
      <li>Redirected to dashboard</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Session Management</h2>

    <p>Your session is stored securely:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>JWT token stored in secure httpOnly cookie</li>
      <li>Session timeout: 30 days (configurable)</li>
      <li>Multiple simultaneous logins allowed</li>
      <li>View and manage active sessions</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="note">
      <strong>🔐 Security Tip:</strong> Always log out after using a shared computer. Check your active sessions regularly for suspicious activity.
    </div>

    <h2>API Authentication</h2>

    <div class="warning">
      <strong>There is no JWT or bearer token.</strong> An earlier version of this
      page described <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code>; the API has never
      accepted it. Logging in creates a server-side <em>session</em> and sets a
      cookie, and programmatic access uses an <code>X-API-Key</code> header. A
      bearer header is ignored and the request is treated as unauthenticated.
    </div>

    <h3>Signing in (session cookie)</h3>
    <p>
      <code>POST /auth/login</code> creates a session and returns the cookie that
      identifies it. <code>-c</code> saves it to a jar so later calls can reuse it:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{loginCurl}</code></pre>
    <pre><code>{sessionCurl}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Sessions are stored server-side, so they can be listed and revoked —
      see <code>GET /auth/sessions</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>Programmatic access (API key)</h3>
    <p>
      For scripts and integrations, create a key under <strong>Integrations</strong>
      and send it on every request. Keys carry scopes and can be restricted by IP
      and by object:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{apiKeyCurl}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Full details, including scopes and rotation, are in the developer docs under
      <a href="/api/authentication">API Authentication</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Remember Me</h2>

    <p>The "Remember Me" option extends your session:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Extended session duration: 90 days</li>
      <li>Requires additional verification after 30 days</li>
      <li>Only use on personal devices</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="warning">
      <strong>⚠️ Warning:</strong> Don't use "Remember Me" on shared computers.
    </div>

    <div class="success">
      <strong>✓ Authenticated!</strong> You're logged in. For more info, see <a href="/user-guide/password-reset">Password Reset & Security</a>.
    </div>
  </div>
</MainLayout>
